# The Hive Queen · the last of the Formics

The Formics — called 'Buggers' by the humans who feared them — are a hive species: their drones are bound philotically to a queen who is the species' true mind. A single distributed consciousness spread across countless bodies, the Hive Queen does not merely command her people; she is them, thinking and acting as one across the whole reach of her bound forms.

The Formics attacked Earth twice and never grasped that each individual human was a sentient self — a fatal misunderstanding of varelse versus raman, of which beings can be reasoned with and which cannot. Ender's fleet answers that misjudgment with extermination. Yet one queen survives, dormant within a cocoon, able to speak mind-to-mind with Ender across the silence. She entrusts him with her revival, and his book 'The Hive Queen' tells her people's side of the war that ended them.

Her nature of emergence is ethereal: she is the philotic made flesh, a connective and collective mind rather than a single embodied self. Where individual bodies move, it is the unseen binding between them — the queen's distributed consciousness — that is the real being. In her, the connective principle of the saga becomes a person, and a survivor.
